Follow up on my question. I managed to read the Lavaan tutorial PDF (https://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/tutorial.pdf) and got an idea of what 'NA' and 'start()' in the equations are. Since Lavaaan by default assumes that the factor loading of the first indicator as 1, we multiply the indicator by NA to tell Lavaan that the factor loading is not necessarily 1 but can be any value. I am not sure if I understood 'start()' fully. But based on what I understood: when the factor loading is specified as free (by multiplying by NA), Lavaan tries a range of values to see what fits best. We can ask Lavaan to start with a specific value and not to try any value below that. Is that correct?

So, as I vaguely understand what NA and start() mean, I'll narrow my questions down. How do you know what values to put inside start()? Is it okay if we don't use start() and let Lavaan figure out the value on its own?

