Ah, I seen your detailed post contained answers to all my questions ;)

1. I don't think in any one situation one model it better then the other, in fact one of the two "residual" models is exactly the same as the cholesky:

model_res <- "model1 <- 'CP  ~ B*SNP

            EA ~ CP + A*SNP"

is equal to:

model<-'C=~NA*EA +start(0.2)*EA + start(0.5)*CP

         NC=~NA*EA +start(0.2)*EA

         C~B*SNP

         NC~ A*SNP

         NC~~1*NC

         C~~1*C

         C~~0*NC

         CP ~~ 0*EA

         CP~~0*CP

         EA~~0*EA

         SNP~~SNP'

Where B should be B and A should be A.

the GWIS model was mainly developed as some point to show in Grotzinger et al that genomicSEM is a versatile tool that can also perfrom"GWIS".

The GWIS models are themselfs in turn transformations of each-other I believe (harder to figure out). Currently I would recommend people use the Cholesky, its a more natural representation (both parameters estimated in 1 model), and it has a lot of empirical work (see our paper) as suport for the validity of Cog and NonCog (where NonCog is the non-cognitive contribution to education specifically.). But the bottom line is all 3 models are representations of the same underlying data.

If you want to share the goal of your study (either here or privately in an email to me and Elliot) we can help you pick the best parameterization.

Best,

Michel

